Trapping $\left[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}\right]^{3-}$ clusters into pre-synthesized ZIF-67 toward $M\circ_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C}}$ particles Confined in Uniform Carbon Polyhedron for Efficient Overall Water Splitting
Bi-transition metal carbides (BTMCs) are promising in catalytic fields, but the synthesis of small-sized BTMCs remains a challenge. Here, the ${\mathsf{M o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C}}$ (main below $20\mathsf{n m}$ ) confined in uniform carbon polyhedron $(\mathsf{M o}_{\mathsf{X}}\mathsf{C o}_{\mathsf{X}}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{C})$ was synthesized based on trapping $\left[\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}\right]^{3-}$ $(\mathsf{P M o}_{12})$ clusters into pre-synthesized, uniform ZIF-67 (PMo/ZIF-67). The opening of window $(0.34\mathsf{n m})$ ) of ZIF-67 cages by heating is essential to allow trapping of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into cages. The trapping route provides a new way to successfully combine POMs and MOFs those can not be effectively combined by traditional POMOF-based (the simultaneous assembly) route. It have also the advantages in controlling the uniformity and components of materials. The size matching of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ (1nm) and cages $(1.16\mathsf{n m})$ ) of ZIF-67 makes an effective contact of Co and Mo source, thus giving small-sized ${\mathsf{M o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C}}$ protected by carbon under calcination. The optimized catalysts showed good performance for water splitting with low η10 of $83~\mathsf{m V}$ $(295~\mathsf{m V})$ for hydrogen (oxygen) evolution reaction, superior to those derived from ZIF-67 and precursor from POMOF-based route. Our results also indicated that HER activity is determined by the kind of BTMCs, and activity for OER is relative with the oxygen-containing  species formed during the initial OER test.
## Introduction
Transition metal carbides (TMCs) $(\mathsf{M o C},^{1,2}\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C}^{3}$ etc) are of promising in catalytic fields. Compared with mono-TMCs (MTMCs), the Bi-TMCs (BTMCs), composed of early TMs (Mo, W etc.) and late TMs (Co, Ni etc.), have shown tuned electronic properties, improved activity and stability in catalysis,4-6 thus received intensive concern. The synthesis of smallsized BTMCs is important for their application, but it is still a challenging task. One of basic routes to form BTMCs is the calcination of a precursor contained ETMs (Mo, W), LTMs (Co, Ni) and carbon sources (pechinibased method).6,7 The route is straight-forward, but suffered from the formation of impure phase MTMCs. In other route, the bi-transition metal oxides have been firstly synthesized followed by calcination.4,8,9 The BTMCs with good purity can be obtained, but usually have a large size up to $\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . The issues are mainly ascribed to uncontrollable distance of two kinds of TMs and absence of an effective protection. The fabrication of small-sized BTMCs with a avoiding to form MTMCs, is important to promote its application in catalytic field (the increase of active sites and/or decrease of their usage).
The accurate control of distance of LTMs and ETMs to realize their effective contact in confined (protective) space is crucial to form smallsized BTMCs. The keggin type polyoxometalates (POMs) $(\mathsf{I}\mathsf{X}\mathsf{M}_{12}\mathsf{O}_{40}]^{\mathsf{n}-}$ $(\mathsf{X}\mathsf{M}_{12},$ X is P, Si etc., M is Mo, W etc.)10,11 is useful Mo (W) sources based on their small size (about $1\mathsf{n m}$ ) and stable structure. Our group has synthesized small-sized MTMCs (nitrides, phosphide) based on confined transformation of POMs on carbon.12-14 If POMs (typically, $\mathsf{P M o}_{12})$ can be confined into LTMs-containing space with matched size, the effective contact of two kinds of TMs can be realized. Metal− organic frameworks (MOFs) have been focus in material science.15,16 The regular cages in MOFs provide useful space for the accommodation of guests.17,18  Thus, the effective combination of POMs and MOFs provides a potential way for carbide synthesis. Traditionally, the combination is realized based on a one-pot “POMOF”-based route, that is, by the simultaneous assembly of metal ions, POMs and ligands. The route is straight-forward, but only suitable to assemble certain ligand (typically, 1,3,5-Benzenetricarboxylic acid), metal ions and POMs. The NENU-5, a POMOF contained ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{P M o}_{12},$ can be prepared with uniform morphology, and used to synthesize MoxC@carbon.19 The pyrolysis of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}@\mathsf{M l L}\cdot100$ (Fe) can from a mixture of $\mathsf{F e}_{3}\mathsf{C}$ and ${\mathsf{M o}}_{2}{\mathsf{C}}$ .20 The result should be relative with un-matched size of MIL-100 cages $(2.5\sim2.9\ \mathrm{nm})$ ) with $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ (1 nm), thus being not favourable for effective contact of Fe and Mo source. Therefore, the size match of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ cluster and cages of MOFs is crucial to realize effective contact of Mo with LTMs. The ZIF-67, composed of Co and 2-MIM (2-methyl imidazole), is potential host due to its matched cage size $(1.16~\mathsf{n m})^{21}$ with $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}\ (1\mathsf{n m}),^{22}$ so that one $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ clusters can only fit into one cage of ZIF-67 in principle. We have attempted to encapsulate $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 cages based on simultaneous assembly of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and 2- MIM. However, the straight-forward route has the difficult in concerting the reaction rate of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ with 2-MIM, thus resulting in uncontrolled assembly (formation of irregular particles) (Figure S1). A new route needed to be developed to realize effective combination of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ with uniform ZIF-67.
Here, we have reported a new, non POMOF-based route to combine $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and ZIF-67 effectively. The route is based on trapping $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into pre-synthesized, uniform ZIF-67. The window aperture of ZIF-67 cage $(0.34\ \mathsf{n m})^{21}$ is too small to allow $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ access. Thus, we have adopted a heating method to make partial disassembly of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM from ZIF-67 in solvent (Scheme 1, Stage 1) (Figure S2). The process can open the window23,24 of ZIF67 cage, thus allowing $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ access (Stage 2). The interaction of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ with 2-MIM on cage is favorable for its stabilization.25 There are presence of dynamic disassembly and reassembly process, just like precipitation-dissolution equilibrium in solid dispersion.26 The reassembly of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM, especially under cooling process, can encapsulate the trapped $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in cage (Stage 3). The trapped amount of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ can be well tuned. Under the calcination, the $_{\mathsf{M o x C o x C}}$ with small size (main below $20\ \mathsf{n m}$ ) in company of Co nanoparticles (NPs) (the Co can be removed by ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ to give $\mathsf{M o x C o}_{\mathsf{x}}\mathsf{C}@\mathsf{C})$ confined in uniform carbon polyhedron $(M\circ_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C}}-$ $\mathsf{C o@C)}$ can be formed benefited from effective contact of Co and Mo source, and the protective role of carbon derived from 2-MIM (Stage 4). The $M\circ_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{X}}{\mathsf{C-C o}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ can be used as active catalyst for hydrogen evolution reaction (HER) and oxygen evolution reaction (OER), with superior performance to catalysts derived from ZIF-67 and the precursor from simultaneous assembly of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ , 2-MIM and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ . The trapping-based route extends largely the kinds of POMs and MOFs those are not suitable to be effectively combined by traditional POMOF-based route, and thus expands the application of POMs and MOFs in material fields. Typically, here, the trapping route provides a robust solution on issues presented in traditional synthesis of BTMCs, although there are several works about the synthesis of MoxC, WC by using MOFs as host.19,27,28
## Results and discussion
The ${\mathsf{M o}}_{\mathsf{x}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{x}}{\mathsf{C}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ is synthesized based on 1) “opening” the window of cages in ZIF-67 under heating, 2) the access of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into opened cage, 3) the encapsulation of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in cage, and 4) the formation of $M\circ_{\times}C\circ_{\times}C-C\circ_{\sqrt{\theta}}C$ by calcinating PMo/ZIF-67 under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmosphere and 5) the removement of Co by ${\mathsf{H}}_{2}{\mathsf{S O}}_{4}$ can give ${\mathsf{M o}}_{\mathsf{x}}{\mathsf{C o}}_{\mathsf{x}}{\mathsf{C}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ . The ZIF-67 have large cage with size of $1.16\ \mathsf{n m}$ . The size of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ is about 1 nm. So, in principle, one cage of ZIF-67 can accommodate one $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ . However, the window aperture (0.34 nm) of cage is too small to allow the access of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into presynthesized ZIF-67. Thus, the opening of window of ZIF-67 cage is a key to allow access of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 cage. There is precipitation-dissolution equilibrium in the dispersion of the solids.26 For MOFs, the process should also exist, and is called as disassembly-reassembly equilibrium. If the disassembly process exhibited, the ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM will be detected in solution. Based on UV-vis test, we have found that the methanol solution of ${\mathsf{C o}}({\mathsf{N O}}_{3})_{2}$ and 2-MIM have shown obvious adsorption peaks in the range of $200-250\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S2a, b). The peak intensity increases with increasing the concentration of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM. So, we have analyzed the disassembly degree based on UV-vis method. To this, the dispersion of ZIF-67 in methanol was heated for certain time. After cooling, the supernatant was separated from methanol solution of ZIF-67. The supernatant shows the obvious absorbance at $\sim220\mathsf{n m}$ , demonstrating the presence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM disassembled from ZIF-67. Also, the supernatant directly separated from hot reaction solution shows higher absorbance than that from cooling solution, implying that the diassembly process can be accelerated at elevated temperature (Figure S2). It also implies the accelerated re-assembly process of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2- MIM under cooling process. The diassembly process will give defect 23,24 in ZIF-67 (open the window of cage). The concentration of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM in solution is about 0.5 mmol ${\mathsf{L}}^{-1}$ by comparing with UV-vis spectra of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ (Figure S2a) and 2-MIM (Figure S2b) solution. The UV-vis  test indicates the partial diassembly of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM from ZIF-67. The removal of a number of linker or metal nodes can not cause collapse of the framework structure due to the high degree of connectivity of MOFs. The disassembly process can open the window of ZIF-67, thus allowing the access of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 cage. The $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ can be stabilized in cage due to their interaction with 2-MIM.25 The reassembly process of Co and 2-MIM, especially under cooling process, on ZIF-67 can make the effective encapsulation of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in ZIF-67 cages (Scheme S1).
The boiling point of methanol is about $64.7^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ at open system under ambient pressure. We have performed the trapping process under solvothermal condition at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to promote the diassembly of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM in ZIF-67, thus realizing more effective trapping of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ . The UV-vis spectra indicate that the peaks of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in solution gradually decrease with prolonging the solvothermal time, implying its trapping in ZIF-67 cages (Figure S2d). One typical sample from $6\mathfrak{h}$ trapping (PMo/ZIF-67-6, Table S1) is characterized by a series of methods to verify the access of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 cage. Thermogravimetric (TG) test shows that weight loss in the range of $30{\cdot}700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ is $46.5\%$ for PMo/ZIF-67-6, which is between $66.5\%$ for ZIF-67 and $22.5\%$ for $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in air, indicating the access of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 (Figure 1a). In infrared (IR) spectroscopy of PMo/ZIF-67-6, a new peak indexing to $\nu$ as (Mo-Ob-Mo) in $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ can be observed, which is absent for ZIF-67. The peak have positive shift of $13c m^{-1}$ in comparison with $\mathsf{P M o}_{12},$ , implying the interaction of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ with 2-MIM at cages. Notably, the nitrogen adsorption/desorption curve shows that both of ZIF-67 and PMo/ZIF-67-6 show same type I isotherms, indicating intactness of the ordered structure of ZIF-67.29 The $S_{B E T}$ value and pore volume are 1755 ${\mathfrak{m}}^{2}/{\mathfrak{g}}.$ , $0.643~{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}/{\mathsf{g}}$ for ZIF-67, and $711\mathsf{m}^{2}/\mathsf{g}$ , $0.262~{\mathsf{c m}}^{3}/{\mathsf{g}}$ for PMo/ZIF-67-6 (Figure 1c) with same pore diameters (Figure 1c). The results are consistent with the encapsulation of guests in porous materials.30 The less surface area of PMo/ZIF-67-6 than ZIF67 provides convincing evidence of successful encapsulation.31 The XRD patterns of PMo/ZIF-67-6 is identical with that of ZIF-67 (Figure 1d), illustrating that $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ is encapsulated into ZIF-67 cage as the separated clusters without altering the crystal parameters of ZIF-67.28,31 A XPS survey spectrum shows the presence of Co, Mo, C, N, O, P in PMo/ZIF-67-6, further implying a combination of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and ZIF-67. In the high-resolution spectrum of the samples, the Mo 3d shows two peaks at 231.8 eV $(3{\mathsf{d}}_{5/2})$ and $234.8\ \mathrm{~eV}$ $(3{\mathsf{d}}5_{3/2})$ (Figure S3). The peaks have shifted to lower energy region in comparison with $\mathsf{P M o}_{12},^{32}$ which should be relative with the partial electronic interaction of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ with ZIF-67 host. Notably, the SEM image shows the uniform polyhedron morphology of PMo/ZIF-67-6 (Figure 2a), being similar with that of ZIF-67 (Figure S4). Based on ICP test, the amount of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in PMo/ZIF-67-6 is about $34.1\%$ . (the calculation detail can be seen in ESI). The tests indicate the effectiveness of “trapping” route to realize encapsulation of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 cages with regular morphology.
Above analyses indicate the successful encapsulation of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 cage. The matched size of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ with cage makes close distance (effective contact) of Co and Mo source, which is crucial to give BTMCs under suitable conditions. Under heating in $\mathsf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere, the 2-MIM can be converted into the carbon and release the reducing gas. In another word, the carbon source is also adjacent with Mo source and Co source, providing further chance for easy formation of BTMCs. The carbon can also protect the particles from aggregation. Here, the PMo/ZIF-67-6 was calcinated under ${\sf N}_{2}$ at $600^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 4h with no use of additional carbon source (PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N). In XRD pattern, the characteristic peaks indexing to ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ (JCPDS 65-8115)5,9 can be seen (Figure S5) but with low intensity. The peaks corresponding to metal Co (JCPDS 15-0806) can also be observed. To solidly prove the existence of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}},$ , we have performed the XRD test on X-ray powder diffractometer with high power. We can see the obvious diffraction peaks of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ in the pattern (Figure S6). The peaks located at $2\ \theta\ =43.1^{\circ}$ , $40.5^{\circ}.$ , $35.8^{\circ}\$ , $32.8^{\circ}$ and $47.2^{\circ}$ and $73.7^{\circ}$ can be indexed to (511), (422), (331), (400), (440) and (822) planes of ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ . The peaks located at 2 $\theta=$ $44.2^{\circ},51.5^{\circ}$ , $75.8^{\circ}$ should be indexed to (111), (200), (220) planes of metal Co. The SEM image of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N shows similar polyhedron morphology (Figure 2b) with ZIF-67-6N (Figure S7). TEM images also show regular polyhedral morphology of PMo/ZIF67-6-6N (Figure 2c, 2d). A magnified image shows the presence of small particles with size mainly below $20\ \mathsf{n m}$ (Figure 2d, e). The high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) shows the interplanar distance of $0.21{\ \mathsf{n m}}$ (Figure 2f), corresponding to (511) plane of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ .33 Notably, there are graphitized carbon (GC) coating on ${\sf M o}_{6}{\sf C o}_{6}{\sf C},$ which can protect ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ from aggregation and benefit fast electron transfer.34 Energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS) elemental mapping confirms the uniform distribution of C, Co, Mo(Figure 2g-j) and P, N (Figure S8) through the polyhedrons. Above results verify the formation of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ and Co confined in uniform polyhedral carbon $(\mathsf{C o}_{6}\mathsf{M o}_{6}\mathsf{C}-\mathsf{C o}@\mathsf{C})$ . The sizes of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ are smaller than that from the carbothermic reduction (up to micrometers)6 and an anion-exchange method (about $50~\mathsf{n m}$ ).5 The formation of ${\sf M o}_{6}{\sf C o}_{6}{\sf C}$ and Co from PMo/ZIF-67-6 can be explained as follows. In PMo/ZIF-67, one cage can only encapsulate one $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ due to matched size of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and ZIF-67 cage, thus giving close distance of Mo and Co source in nanorange. Under the calcination in $\mathsf{N}_{2},$ the carbon source (2-MIM) can react with adjacent Mo and Co to form ${\sf M o}_{6}{\sf C o}_{6}{\sf C}$ . In addition, the number of Co atom is more than Mo atom in one cage units, assuming that all cages of ZIF-67 were filled by $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ (in our trapping route, it is inevitable to exist a part of un-filled cages). So, the Co is excess over Mo. The excessive Co can be reduced to form Co NPs. Under heating, the carbon derived from 2-MIM can be simultaneously formed. Thus, the ${\sf M o}_{6}{\sf C o}_{6}{\sf C}$ and Co encapsulated into the carbon can be formed by the calcination of PMo/ZIF-67 under ${\sf N}_{2}$ at lower temperature. Based on ICP analysis, the content of Co is about $26\%$ in PMo/ZIF-67-6. TG test shows that the amount of carbon in $\mathrm{PMo}_{12}/\mathrm{ZIF}.$ - 67-6-6N is about $9.4\%$ (Figure S9). Our “ trapping ” route is different from POMOF-based route in the synthesis of carbides. The NENU-5 from the simultaneous assembly of ${\mathsf{C u}}^{2+}$ , BTC and $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ is suitable precursor for ${\mathsf{M o}}_{\mathsf{x}}{\mathsf{C}}@{\mathsf{C}}$ but not ${\mathsf{B T M C s}}({\varpi}{\mathsf{C}},^{\mathsf{1}}{\mathsf{\Omega}}$ 9 which should be relative with that the Cu is difficult to form carbide. The BTMCs confined in regular carbon polyhedron is obtained by our route, while irregular carbon contained separated MTMCs were formed in POMOF-based route.20 The ratio of late and early TMs is easy to be controlled by adjusting “trapping” parameters in our syntheses. Also, the N-doped carbon can be formed by carbonizing 2-MIM, which is favourable in providing the active sites for catalysis.34
X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was carried out to further analyze the components of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N. The peaks of Co, Mo, C, N and P elements are observed at $779\ \mathrm{eV}.$ , 231eV, 284eV, 398eV, and $\mathsf{134e V}$ (Figure 3a). In the high-resolution XPS of Mo 3d region (Figure 3c), the peaks located at 232.2 and 228.7 eV are assigned to $\mathsf{M o}3\mathsf{d}_{3/2}$ and $\mathsf{M o}3\mathsf{d}_{5/2}$ of Mo-C bond in $\mathsf{M o}_{6}\mathsf{C o}_{6}\mathsf{C},^{33}$ while the peaks at 235.8 eV and 232.7 eV is related to ${M o}^{6+}$ from the slight surface oxidation of carbides.34, 35 The result is consistent with Mo3d peaks in ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ nanoframe/graphene reported by He et al, for which ${\mathsf{M o}}^{4+}$ and ${M o}^{6+}$ can be observed at 232.3, 233.1 for 3d5/2, and 235.6 and $237.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $3{\mathsf{d}}3/2$ . The peak for Mo-C $(\mathsf{M o}^{2+})$ can be observed at 228.5 eV.33 The $\mathsf{C o}2\mathsf{p}_{3/2}$ core level spectrum can be fitted with four components corresponding to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{0}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ and satellites peaks. The ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ peaks are located at 781.5 eV, $780.2\ \mathrm{eV}$ for $2{\mathsf{p}}_{3/2}$ , and 798.3 eV, 796.4 eV for ${\mathsf{C o}}2{\mathsf{p}}_{1/2}$ , respectively. The peaks corresponding to ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ are well consistent with that in ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ . The presence of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{3+}$ should be ascribed to the slight surface oxidation. The peak at 777.6 and $792.0\ \mathrm{eV}$ is assigned to Co metal $(\mathsf{C o}^{0})$ .36 The XPS analyses suggest the formation of cobalt molybdenum carbides and Co metal in PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N.33, 36 The C1s peak can be divided into seven subunits, including C-P $(285.31\ \mathrm{eV},$ ), C-N (285.90 eV), C-C (284.62 eV), C-O (287.03 eV), $c=0$ (288.23 eV), $0-C=0$ (290.13 eV), $\pi{-}\pi$ (291.90 eV).13 The peak of C-Metal (Mo or Co) can not be observed because it low content in carbon components.In comparison with the previous reports,7,33 the C1s peaks about $0-C=0$ , $c=0$ and C-O in PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N are more higher, which may be due to the surface oxidation of carbon and adsorption of oxygen-containing specie during the storage. High-resolution of N 1s peaks can be divided into three peaks, indexing to the pyridinic-N (398.7 eV), pyrrolic-N $(399.3\ \mathsf{e V})$ and graphitic-N $(402.3\ \mathsf{e V})$ (Figure S10a).13 The N-doped carbon(N-Carbon) should be from the pyrolysis of 2- MIM in PMo/ZIF-67-6. XPS spectrum of P is shown in Figure S10b. After deconvolution, we can see the sub-peaks associated with PO (13.4.2 eV), P-C $(133.2~\mathrm{eV})$ .37 The peaks at 130.4 eV should be ascribed to P-Mo or P-Co.38, 39 Based on $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ structure, the P is surround by Mo, thus it can easily combine with Mo in the products due to close distance of Mo and P. The presence of N and P doped carbon is favourable to provide active sites for catalysis. The XPS results indicate the formation of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}},$ , Co and their encapsulation by N-doped carbon. A comparison of cobalt carbide40 and molybdenum carbide41 shows the positive shift of Co 2p and Mo 3d peaks of ${\sf M o}_{6}{\sf C o}_{6}{\sf C}\ @{\sf C},$ indicating the presence of more electrons of Mo and Co in ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ . The abundant electrons on metal sites should be favourable to improve the catalytic activity.
The Co in PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N can be etched away in ${\sf H}_{2}{\sf S}{\sf O}_{4}$ (denoting as PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N-E or ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}@{\mathsf{C}})$ as shown by XRD (Figure S11). The sample was analyzed by ICP, TEM, EDX and XPS. Based on ICP test, the molar ratio of Co and Mo is about 1.08:1, being close to theoretical value of 1:1 in ${\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ The value is lower than 2.98 (Co/Mo) for PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N, implying the removement of Co NPs. The ratio of Co/Mo from TEM-EDX is about 1.64 (Figure S12). The XPS test shows the ratio of Mo/Co is about 1.83 (Figure S13). The values from XPS and EDS are difference with theoretical value due to their surface analysis characteristics (for example. the intensity of photoelectrons is related to the concentration of the atoms, the average free path of the photoelectron, and the thickness of the carbon shell surround the particles). TEM test of ${\mathsf C o}_{6}{\mathsf M o}_{6}{\mathsf C}@{\mathsf C}$ shows the uniform distribution of small particles encapsulated in carbon polyhedrons, being similar with that of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N. The HRTEM of one particles shows the interplanar distance of $0.21{\mathsf{n m}}$ , which is indexed to (511) plane of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ (Figure S12 b-d).33 The EDS mapping indicates uniform distribution of small particles on carbon polyhedrons (Figure S12). After removing Co NPs, the XPS spectra of $\mathtt{C o2p}$ and Mo3d are consistent with that in $\mathsf{M o}_{6}\mathsf{C o}_{6}\mathsf{C}^{33}$ (Figure S13).
By tuning calcination temperature, the components can be tuned as ${\sf M o}_{6}{\sf C o}_{6}{\sf C}-{\sf C o}\ @{\sf C},$ $M_{0_{3}}C_{0_{3}}C_{-}C_{0}\textcircled{\omega}C$ and ${\mathsf{C o@C}}$ (Figure S14). We found that the higher temperature are favourable to form BTMCs with improved crystallinity. Specifically, the intensive diffraction of $M O_{3}C O_{3}C$ can be observed in PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N and PMo/ZIF-67-6-8N. The temperatures for the formation of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ and $M O_{3}C O_{3}C$ are much lower than in carbothermic reduction route9 and other routes.5 The formation temperature of Mo (W)- based carbides is relative with the kind of carbon source and their contact degree (distance) with metal source based on previous reports. In general, the use of carbon source that can close contact with metal source are favourable to give carbides at lower temperature. For example, the ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ can be obtained by heating the mixture of carbon black and ${\mathsf{C o M o O}}_{4}$ under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmospheres at higher temperature (about $1000^{\circ}\mathsf{C})$ .9 We think that carbon black can not effective contact with ${\mathsf{C o M o O}}_{4}$ due to their lager size, thus resulting the need of high synthetic temperature. In another case, the carbon-containing molecular $(\mathsf{F}127^{42}$ , dicyandiamide43) were firstly combined with Mo and Co source. These carbon source can effective contact with metal precursor due to their polymer characteristics. As a results, the BTMCs $(F e_{3}\mathsf{W}_{3}\mathsf{C},^{42}\mathsf{C o}_{6}\mathsf{W}_{6}\mathsf{C}^{43})$ can be formed at lower temperature (below 800 $^{\circ}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{)}}$ . So, it is proposed that a effective contact of carbon source with metal precursor should be key to form carbides at lower temperature. In our synthesis, the 2-MIM at ZIF-67 cages play the role of carbon source. The $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ was confined into the cage. The Co source are coordinated with 2-MIM. So, the carbon source can effective contact with both Mo and Co source in nano-range. Therefore, the MoxCoxC can be obtained at low temperature in our synthesis. A series of experiments demonstrate that the trapping amount of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ in ZIF-67 increase with the increase of solvethermal time. The trapping of too much $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ can result in the damage of the structure of ZIF-67 (Figure S15), just like the blast of balloon as filling too much gases. Also, the use of ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{M o O}}_{4}$ as Mo source can result in damage of ZIF-67 morphology (Figure S16), and formation of mixture of MoC, $\mathsf{C o}_{2}\mathsf{C}$ and $M o_{3}C o_{3}C$ after calcination (Figure S17). The results are related with the weak interaction of ${\mathsf{N a}}_{2}{\mathsf{M o O}}_{4}$ with 2-MIM and its unmatched size with ZIF-67 cage.
${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ in 1M KOH for OER. c) and f) are polarization curves of PMo/ZIF-67-6- 6N (HER) and PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N (OER) initially and after 1000 cycles of CV sweeping, respectively. The insets in c) and $\mathsf{f})$ is i-t curves for PMo/ZIF-67-6- 6N and PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N for 22 h. g) Polarization curves of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N as HER electrode and PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N as OER electrode for overall water splitting in 1.0 M KOH. h)The catalytic stability for overall water splitting at 1.67 V for 100h. i) The photograph for the overall water splitting cell.
The small size of BTMCs and protection by carbon are favorable for their application as electrocatalyst. The activity for HER was evaluated in a three-electrode system in 1M KOH with IR correction. As shown in Figure 4a, all samples exhibit the obvious activity towards HER, and the $\mathsf{P t/C}$ shows outstanding behavior. As comparison, the $\mathsf{C o@}$ carbon samples are also prepared by calcination of ZIF-67 at 600 (ZIF-67-6N). It is shown that the ZIF-67- 6N needs an overpotential $(\mathrm{\textit{\eta}}10)$ of $169~\mathsf{m V}$ , whereas PMo/ZIF67-6-6N only requires an $\eta10$ of $83~\mathsf{m V}$ . At the current density of 100 mA $\mathsf{c m}^{-2}.$ , the overpotentials ( $\textit{n}100)$ of ZIF-67-6N and PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N are $303~\mathsf{m v}$ and $202~\mathsf{m V}.$ , respectively. It is obvious that PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N can promote HER activity largely. The activity of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N is lower than noble metal-based catalyst (such as $\mathsf{R u}/\mathsf{C}^{44})$ , but is comparable, even higher than most Mo- and Co-based catalysts at high current density (Table S2) Tafel slope for PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N is about $50~\mathrm{mV~dec}^{-1}$ , much lower than that of ZIF-67-6N $(88\ \mathsf{m V}\ \mathsf{d e c}^{-1})$ ) (Figure 4b), suggesting the Volmer-Heyrovsky mechanism of HER on the catalyst.45 In accelerated degradation test, there is no obvious loss of current density after 1000 cycles (Figure 4c). In the chronoamperometry (CA) test (inset in Figure 4c), the current density has negligible change after the operation for $22\ \mathsf{\textsf{h}}$ . The good stability is associated with protection by N-doped carbon. We further measured electrochemical surface area (ECSAs) of the catalysts based on the electrochemical double layer capacitance (EDLC, Cdl) (Figure S18). The Cdl values of PMo/ZIF-67-600 is $27.32~\mathsf{m F}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}.$ being higher than $15.58~\mathsf{m F}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ of ZIF-67-6N. The higher ECSA of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N leads to its large exchange current density (0.67 mA $\mathsf{c m}^{-2};$ . The HER activity of catalysts in alkaline medium is related with their structure.46, 47 Here, the synthetic parameters have large effect on the structure , and, consequently, on the activity of catalysts (Figure S19, Table S3-S6). For example, low trapping amount of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and low calcination temperature can result in the low amount of BTMCs in catalysts, thus decreasing the catalytic activity. In contrast, the high trapping amount of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and/or high calcination temperature make the increase of particle size of carbides, thus giving low activity. The good activity of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N should be relative with 1) the small size of BTMCs favoring the exposure of abundant available active sites,4 2) the N-doped carbon coating for stabilizing the carbides,34 and the synergistic electronic effect between them,49 4) the porous structure to facilitate the transfer of reagent (Figure S20). Also, the PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N shows work function (WF) of $5.68\mathsf{e V}$ (Figure S21a). The WF of ZIF-67-6N is about 5.78 eV (Figure S21c). The PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N give work function more close to Pt (Figure S21b), implies the enhanced trapping ability to electrons after surface activation.
The HER catalysts need to match with OER catalysts for the overall water splitting. We further evaluated OER activity of different catalysts. In this case, the PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N from calcination of PMo/ZIF-67-6 at $700^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ exhibits superior activity to other catalysts for OER (Figure 4d and Figure S22). The Co@carbon, prepared by calcination of ZIF-67 at 600 (ZIF-67-7N), and ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ are used as comparison. The $\eta10$ and $\eta\ 100$ for PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N are 295 and $370~\mathrm{mV}$ respectively, lower than 350 and $440~\mathsf{m V}$ for ZIF67-7N. The performance is also superior to $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}$ , which have $\eta10$ of $324~\mathsf{m V}$ and $\eta\ 100$ of $564~\mathsf{m V}$ . Tafel plot of PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N is about $35~\mathsf{m V}~\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ , while it is $39~\mathsf{m V}~\mathsf{d e c}^{-1}$ and $66~\mathsf{m V}$ dec−1 for ZIF67-7N and $\mathsf{R u O}_{2},$ respectively. The PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N shows better performance than catalysts from other synthetic parameters (Table S8-S10), and better or comparable activity with most OER catalysts (Table S11). The Cdl value of PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N $_{(47.4\mathsf{m}\mathsf{F}}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{-2})$ is larger than that of ZIF-67-7N $(9.0~\mathsf{m F}~\mathsf{c m}^{-2}.$ ) (Figure S23). After 1000 CV cycles, there is no obvious change in LSV (Figure 4f). The i−t curve obtained with a stationary potential of $\boldsymbol{1.53\vee}$ for 22 h further identify the good stability of PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N (inset in Figure 4f). The activity of PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N $(\mathsf{M o}_{3}\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{C-C o}@\mathsf{C})$ should be relative with the surface activation by surface oxidation and their specific structure.50 The Faradic efficiency (FE) values are about $100\%$ for HER and $93\%$ for OER at 60 min of electrolysis (Figure S24), being close to the values in previous report.14, 51
The superiority of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N for HER and PMo/ZIF-67-6- 7N for OER is noteworthy. For HER, the carbides is considered as active components and the activity is relative with their kinds (structure). Tao et al. Have indicated that the ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ are more active than ${\mathsf{M o}}_{3}{\mathsf{C o}}_{3}{\mathsf{C}}$ .33 The main carbide phase is ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ in PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N and $M O_{3}C O_{3}C$ in PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N. So, the PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N can show a better activity than PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N. After etching away of Co NPs, the catalytic performance of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N for HER have no obvious change, verifying the main role of ${\mathsf{M o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C o}}_{6}{\mathsf{C}}$ for HER (Figure S25). While for OER, the containing-oxygen species formed during the initial OER test are considered active components for OER.52 For example, Xu et al. have indicated that NiOOH layer formed on Nickel nitride during the OER test should be active species.53 Similar phenomenon can also be observed for ${\sf N i}_{2}{\sf P}/{\sf F e}_{2}{\sf P}$ and $\mathsf{C o}_{4}\mathsf{N}$ system.54,50 The difference in components of PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N and PMo/ZIF-67-6- 7N (the kind of bi-metal carbides and content of Co etc.) will effect the formation and micro-structure of containing-oxygen species, thus resulting in different catalytic ability. In our catalyst, Co in catalyst can be easily oxidized in KOH under OER conditions to form $\mathsf{C o O x}$ species, which is verified to be active for OER reaction.55 We have performed the XPS and XRD analyses for the catalyst after $40~\mathsf{C V}$ cycles of OER test. The XRD and XPS analyses show the existence of ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ in company with the low peaks of Co metal, indicating the oxidation of Co in PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N (Figure S26, S27). The ${\mathsf{C o}}(\mathsf{O H})_{2}$ can be oxidized during OER to form CoOx species those are active species for OER.56 Although the peaks corresponding to Mo species can not be seen after OER test in XRD, the XPS shows the presence of amorphous MoOx species (Figure S28).57 The presence of Mo species is important for enhanced OER activity. Typically, after etching away of Co NPs from PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N, the catalytic activity of materials $(\mathsf{M o}_{3}\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{C}@$ carbon) have a decrease for OER, indicating the contribution of Co NPs in PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N on OER. (Figure S29). In addition, the samples from calcination of ZIF-67-7N $\mathsf{C o@}$ carbon) have also shows worse OER activity than PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N (Figure 4). Zhang et al. have indicated that the presence of W species is important to enhance OER activity.58 Our study shows that Mo species is also important for OER. The PMo/ZIF-67-6-7N composed of Co and $M O_{3}C O_{3}C$ shows better OER performance than $\mathsf{M o}_{3}\mathsf{C o}_{3}\mathsf{C}_{\mathsf{e}}$ carbon and $\mathsf{C o@}$ carbon. The enhanced OER activity should be ascribed to the synergetic effect of Co and $_{{\sf M o}_{\sf x}{\sf C o}_{\sf x}{\sf C}}$ on the formation of active species during OER test.
A two-electrode system was assembled by using PMo/ZIF-67- 6-7N as anode and PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N as cathode in 1.0 M KOH for overall water splitting. As shown in Figure ${4}\mathrm{g},$ to reach the current density of $10\mathsf{m A}\mathsf{c m}^{-2}$ , the potential needed is about $\displaystyle1.61\mathrm{\sfV}.$ . The performance is comparable with the most non-precious metal catalysts, for example, ${\mathsf{N i}}/{\mathsf{M o}}_{2}{\mathsf{C}}{\mathsf{-P C}}^{59}$ (Table S12). The i−t curves also show good stability of electrodes after continual test of $100{\mathsf{h}}$ (Figure 4h). The bubbles can generate on both electrodes at this potential (Figure 4i, Figure S30).
## Conclusions
We have developed a trapping-based, non POMOF strategy to combine $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and ZIF-67 effectively. The size match of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ and ZIF-67 is makes a effective contact of them, thus resulting in the formation of BTMCs under calcination. The N-doped carbon derived from 2-MIM ligand at cages can protect particles from server aggregation, thus obtaining $_{{\sf M o}_{\sf x}C o_{\sf x}C}$ with much smaller size than that from previous routes. Benefiting from small size of $_{{\sf M o}_{\sf x}{\sf C o}_{\sf x}{\sf C}}$ and its protection by N-carbon, the materials could be used as superior catalysts for overall water splitting. The “trapping” route have superiority to POMOF-based route in terms of acquiring $_{{\sf M o}_{\sf x}{\sf C o}_{\sf x}{\sf C}}$ confined in uniform carbon with improved catalytic performance (Figure S31-34). The trappingbased route extends the kinds of POMs and MOFs those can not be effectively combined by POMOF-based route. It provides a new avenue to combine POMs and MOFs effectively, thus expanding their application in material fields.
## Experimental  procedures
## Chemicals and the Preparation of catalysts
Cobalt nitrate hexahydrate $\cos(N O_{3})_{2}.$ · $6H_{2}O$ , 99wt $\%$ ) and 2- methylimidazole $(\mathsf{C}_{4}\mathsf{H}_{6}\mathsf{N}_{2},\mathsf{\Omega}98\%)$ were obtained from Aladdin Ltd. Phosphomolybdic acid hydrate $({\mathsf{H}}_{3}{\mathsf{M o}}_{12}{\mathsf{O}}_{40}{\mathsf{P}}^{\prime}$ · $\mathsf{x H}_{2}\mathsf{O}\geq\mathsf{99}.9$ wt $\%$ ) was purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. Methanol anhydrous $(C H_{3}O H,\ge99.5$ wt $\%$ ) was purchased from Tianjin Guangfu Fine Chemical Research Institute. Pt/C $(20\%)$ was obtained from Shanghai Hesen electric corporation. Sodium molybdate dihydrate $(\mathsf{N a}_{2}\mathsf{M o O}_{4}\cdot2\mathsf{H}_{2}\mathsf{O},\textsf{\tiny299}\textsf{v}$ t $\%$ ) was obtained from Tianjin Kermel Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. ${\mathsf{R u O}}_{2}$ was purchased from Alfa Aesar. There is no further purification of all the reagents and solvent.
## Synthesis of ZIF-67
The ZIF-67 polyhedrons were synthesized according to the previous report with tiny variation.60 Typically, 12 mmol of $C\circ(N O_{3})_{2}\cdot6H_{2}O$ was first dissolved in $300~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of methanol. The 2- MIM $(48\:m m o l)$ ) was added into another $300~\mathrm{mL}$ of methanol. The former solution was poured into the latter and then stirred for 6h at room temperature $(\sim25^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ . The purple precipitate was collected by filtration and washed with methanol repeatedly. Asprepared purple solids were dried at $60^{\circ}\textsf{C}$ in a vacuum oven for $12\mathsf{h}$ to give the final product ZIF-67.
## Synthesis of PMo/ZIF-67-6
The trapping of $\mathsf{P M o}_{12}$ into ZIF-67 was performed in methanol solvent under solvothermal condition, and corresponding samples were denoted as PMo/ZIF-67-X (X represents the solvothermal time). Typical sample, PMo/ZIF-67-6, was obtained through followed procedure. The 0.452 mmol of ZIF-67 (0.1g) was dispersed in $30~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol and stirred at $30^{\circ}\textsf{C}$ for 12h (solution A). The stirring for long time at this temperance can make the partial diassembly of ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ and 2-MIM from ZIF-67, thus giving defects to open the window of pore. The ${\mathsf H}_{3}{\mathsf P}{\mathsf M}{\mathsf o}_{12}{\mathsf O}_{40}$ $(0.05\mathrm{g},0.330\ \mathsf{m m o l})$ ) was dissolved in $30~\mathsf{m L}$ of methanol (solution B). The solution B is poured into the solution A quickly under constantly stirring. The mixture was stirred for $30\mathrm{\min}$ and then transferred into a $100~\mathsf{m L}$ Teflon-lined autoclave, and treated at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^\textrm{\scriptsize6h}$ . The resulting solid was isolated by filtration, and was washed with methanol at least three times. The solid was dried at $60^{\circ}\textsf{C}$ in a vacuum oven for 12h (denoted as PMo/ZIF-67-
6, where the 6 represents the solvothermal time). The synthesis procedure of other control samples is given in ESI.
## Synthesis of PMo/ZIF-67-6-yN
In a typical procedure, the PMo/ZIF-67-6 $\mathrm{50~mg}$ ) was placed in a porcelain boat. The boat was heated from room temperature to $600^{\circ}~\mathsf{C}$ at the rate of $2^{\circ}\mathrm{C}/\mathsf{m i n}$ and held at ${600}^{\circ}$ °C for 4 hours under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atomspheres. After cooling down to room temperature naturally, the black power can be formed, which was denoted as PMo/ZIF-67-6-6N (6N represent calcination at $600^{\circ}\textsf{C}$ under $\ensuremath{\mathsf{N}}_{2})$ ). The PMo/ZIF-67-6 was also calcined at 500, 700, $800^{\circ}$ °C under ${\sf N}_{2}$ atmospheres to tune the component and micro-structure of the samples. The samples were named as PMo/ZIF-67-6-5N, PMo/ZIF67-6-7N, PMo/ZIF-67-6-8N, respectively.
## Characterizations of the products
Scanning electron microscopy (SEM) test was carried out on Hitachi S-4800 instrument at an accelerating voltage of $5~{\mathsf{k V}}$ . The transmission electron microscopy (TEM) and high-resolution TEM (HRTEM) were analyzed by JEM-2100 electron microscope (JEOL, Japan) with an acceleration voltage of $200~\mathsf{k V}$ . The EDS mapping was taken by EM-F200(TF-HR) equipped with JED-2300T  Large Angle Dual windowsless SDD-EDS, and FEI TF20 equipped withOxford energy spectrometer. X-Ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on Bruke D8 equipped with $\mathsf{c u-K}\alpha$ radiation (λ $=1.5406\mathrm{~\AA~}_{\cdot}$ ), and a Rigaku D/max-2600/PC X-ray diffractometer (XRD, $\mathsf{c u-K}\alpha$ radiation). X-ray photon spectroscopy (XPS) analysis was collected on a VG ESCALABMK II with Mg kα radiation (1253.6 eV). A PE Spectrum One B IR spectrometer was used to record the fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) spectrometer of the samples in the region of $400{\cdot}4000~\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ with KBr pellets. Thermogravimetric (TG) analysis was performed on TA Q600 thermal analyzer under a stream of air with a heating rate of $\boldsymbol{10}^{\circ}$ $\mathsf{c}\cdot\mathsf{m i n}^{-1}$ . Nitrogen adsorption-desorption isotherms were measured at $77~\mathsf{\textsf{K}}$ on a Quantachrome autosorb iQ/ASiQwin. Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) method was used to calculate the specific surface area and the Density-Functional-Theory (DFT) model was used to calculate the pore size distribution. Ahead of the experiment, the samples were degassed at $150^{\circ}~\mathsf{C}$ for $6\mathfrak{h}$ in vacuum. UV – vis absorption spectra were recorded on UV1600/1800B at room temperature. The ICP test was performed in ICP-OES8000 analyzer.
The work function was calculated based on the Scanning Kelvin Probe (SKP) test (SKP5050 system, Scotland) which was performed at normal laboratory conditions (in ambient atmosphere). The reference electrode used in the test is a gold electrode. The work function ${\bigl(}\phi{\bigr)}$ is calculated by the following formula :
$$
{\varphi_{\mathrm{Au}}}-\ {\frac{\boldsymbol{\eta}_{\mathrm{Au}}}{1000}}\ =\ \varphi-\frac{\boldsymbol{\eta}}{1000}
$$
In the formula, $\phi\mathsf{A}\mathsf{u}$ is the work function of Au, $\eta\mathsf{A u}=-239.75$ eV.
## Electrochemical Measurements
The electrochemical experiments towards HER and OER were conducted on BAS100B electrochemical workstation at room temperature. The measurements were taken with a standard three electrode in 1M KOH. The glassy carbon electrode (GCE,1mm in diameter) was used as the working electrode, an ${\sf A g/A g C l}$ (filled with saturated KCl) and graphite rod were used as the reference electrode and counter electrode respectively. Prior to the measurements, three kinds of alumina powder $(1.5\:\mu\:\mathsf{m},0.5$ $\mu\mathsf{m}$ and $0.05\mu\mathrm{~m})$ were used to polish GCE. To obtain the catalyst ink, $5~\mathrm{mg}$ of catalyst was dispersed in $1~\mathsf{m L}$ water/ethanol mixture $\scriptstyle(\mathsf{v}/\mathsf{v}=1:4)$ . Then 20 $\mu\mathsf{L}$ Nafion (5 wt $\%$ ) was added into the mixture. The homogeneous catalyst ink was formed after the ultrasonication. The well-dispersed ink $(10~\mu\ L)$ was pipetted onto the GCE and dried under a heating lamp. The loading of the catalyst is $0.708\mathsf{m g\ c m^{-2}}$ . The commercial $20\%\mathsf{P t/C}$ catalyst was chosen as a reference. Linear sweep voltammetrys (LSV) were tested on GCE coated with catalyst under 1600 rpm at the rate of $\mathsf{S}\mathsf{m}\mathsf{V}\mathsf{s}^{-1}$ with IR compensation. The Tafel data are fitted according to Tafel equation $\eta={\tt b l o g(j)}+\alpha$ (j: current density, b: Tafel slope). The electrochemical active surface areas (ESCA) of the modified electrodes were calculated based on the double-layer capacitor under the potential window of $0.1{\cdot}0.2\mathrm{~V~}$ (HER）and 1.2-1.3V (OER) vs. RHE. The scan rate of cyclic voltammograms (CVs) were varied from 40 to $220\mathsf{m}\mathsf{v}^{-1}$ . Chronoamperometry (CA) tests were carried out at $-100~\mathsf{m v}$ vs RHE (HER) and 1.53V (OER) vs. RHE. The 1000 CV cycles at the scan rate of $100\mathrm{\mV}\cdot\textsf{s}^{-1}$ were performed to valuate the stability of the catalysts. There is no change in PH before and after the test. In order to convert the the potential to the reversible hydrogen electrode (RHE), the Nernst equation was presented:ER $\mathsf{\mathsf{I E}}=\mathsf{E A g}/\mathsf{A g C l}\ +\ 0.059\ \mathsf{p H}\ +\mathsf{E}^{\theta}\mathsf{_{A g/A g C l}}$ . The PH of the electrolyte is 13.96.
The Faradic efficiency (FE) for GER and OER was calculated by comparing the experimentally determined and theoretically calculated amounts of ${\sf H}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{O}_{2}$ . In test, a certain amount of catalyst ink was dropped on Ni foam $(2.53\ m g\ c m^{-2})$ and dried in air as electrode. Potentiostatic electrolysis was performed at current density of $100~\mathsf{m A}$ $\mathsf{c m}^{2-}$ for 60 minutes in the home-made airtight H-type electrolytic cell. The amount of gas generated was analyzed by gas chromatography (GC). The theoretical ${\sf H}_{2}$ amount were calculated as follows:
